    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q23-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the three-digit number that increases by 30% when each of its digits is increased by 1.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$370$.\n\nIncreasing each digit by 1 adds $111$, which is $30\\%$ of the original number.",
          "worked": "Let $x=100a+10b+c$ be the original three-digit number. Increasing each digit by 1 gives\n$$\\begin{align*}100(a+1)+10(b+1)+(c+1)&=100a+100+10b+10+c+1\\\\100(a+1)+10(b+1)+(c+1)&=(100a+10b+c)+111\\\\100(a+1)+10(b+1)+(c+1)&=x+111.\\end{align*}$$\nA $30\\%$ increase gives $130\\%$ of the original number, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+111}{x}&=\\frac{13}{10}\\\\10x+1110&=13x\\\\3x&=1110\\\\x&=370.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: increasing the digits of $370$ gives $481$, and $1.3\\times370=481$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q23-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that no three-digit number increases by 40% when each of its digits is increased by 1.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such number exists.\n\nIncreasing each digit by 1 adds $111$. A $40\\%$ increase would require the original number to be $277.5$, which is not an integer.",
          "worked": "Let $x$ be the original three-digit number. Increasing each digit by 1 gives $x+111$. A $40\\%$ increase gives $140\\%$ of the original number, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+111}{x}&=\\frac{14}{10}\\\\\\frac{x+111}{x}&=\\frac{7}{5}\\\\5x+555&=7x\\\\2x&=555\\\\x&=277.5.\\end{align*}$$\nA three-digit number must be an integer. Since $555$ is not divisible by $2$, no such number exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      }
    ]
  },
